QRCode.mcc/QRCode.mcc

   QRCode.mcc is a Zune custom class derived from MUIC_Area. It encodes
   caller-supplied text or binary data as a QR Code Model 2 symbol and draws
   its modules directly through the AROS graphics API.

   The object copies input synchronously when created or updated. Drawing
   does not rerun the encoder. The symbol uses integer pixel scaling and
   four light modules of quiet zone on every side.

   When the object is smaller than the complete symbol, it draws no QR Code.
   Status reports the encoding result, not the size or contrast of the
   resulting screen image.

   The input payload may contain short-lived credentials such as login URLs.
   Clearing the content with NULL/empty text or an empty data payload clears
   the displayed symbol and releases its stored input buffer. The class does
   not securely erase released memory. Applications own expiration and
   access policy; do not rely on clearing the gadget to destroy secrets.

   The encoder is derived from Project Nayuki's MIT-licensed QR Code generator.
   Its original license notice is retained in qrcodegen.c and qrcodegen.h.
QRCode.mcc/MUIA_QRCode_Text

   MUIA_QRCode_Text -- [IS.], CONST_STRPTR

   Sets a NUL-terminated UTF-8 byte string. The object immediately copies up
   to 7089 input bytes. A NULL pointer or empty string clears the QR Code.
   Oversized or unterminated input reports Invalid and clears the old symbol.

   The application is responsible for supplying valid UTF-8. The class
   copies the bytes without validating their Unicode encoding and does not
   translate a system-local character set. Set either Text or Data in
   one tag transaction, never both.
QRCode.mcc/MUIA_QRCode_Data

   MUIA_QRCode_Data -- [IS.], const struct MUI_QRCodePayload *

   Encodes an arbitrary byte sequence, up to 2953 bytes. The structure has
   members Bytes (const UBYTE *) and Length (ULONG). The object immediately
   copies the bytes and does not retain the structure pointer.

   A NULL structure or a zero-length payload clears the symbol. For nonzero
   Length, Bytes must be non-NULL and must reference at least Length bytes.
   The caller retains ownership of the structure and the source bytes.
   Set either Text or Data in one tag transaction, never both.
QRCode.mcc/MUIA_QRCode_ECC

   MUIA_QRCode_ECC -- [ISG], ULONG

   Selects the error-correction level: MUIV_QRCode_ECC_L,
   MUIV_QRCode_ECC_M (default), MUIV_QRCode_ECC_Q, or MUIV_QRCode_ECC_H.
   The specified level is used exactly; the encoder never raises it
   automatically. Changing ECC regenerates the QR Code from the saved
   input. An invalid level reports Invalid; content that cannot fit
   at the selected level reports NoCapacity. Both clear the symbol.
   The getter returns the last supplied ECC value, even if that value
   is outside the supported range.
QRCode.mcc/MUIA_QRCode_Status

   MUIA_QRCode_Status -- [..G], ULONG

   Reports the current encoding outcome:

     MUIV_QRCode_Status_Empty       No current input
     MUIV_QRCode_Status_Ready       Valid encoded matrix
     MUIV_QRCode_Status_Invalid     Invalid parameters or input
     MUIV_QRCode_Status_NoMemory    Allocation failed
     MUIV_QRCode_Status_NoCapacity  Input does not fit requested ECC

   A non-Ready status never retains the previous QR symbol. Failed input
   updates do not restore previous content. Encoding failures are reported
   through this attribute, not the return value of OM_SET. Status_Ready
   does not assert that the gadget has enough display pixels to render it.
